Done.

**Bug:** The old `extractTags` split on spaces and checked `hasPrefix("#")`. A `#` tag embedded in punctuation (e.g. `#urgent.` after a comma, or `(#ideas)`) was never space-delimited and never started with `#`, so it was dropped.

**Fix:** Rewrote `extractTags` as a single-pass scanner. On `#` it starts collecting; it accumulates the following run of tag characters (letters, digits, `_`) and stops at any non-tag character (punctuation, whitespace). Tags are appended in encounter order.

**Verified** (compiled and run against the source logic):
- `hello #world #swift` → `["world", "swift"]` (existing test, unchanged)
- `just plain text` → `[]` (existing test, unchanged)
- `shopping list,#urgent` → `["urgent"]` ✓
- `(#ideas` → `["ideas"]` ✓
- order preserved: `#b,#a #c,#d` → `["b","a","c","d"]` ✓
- trailing punctuation / digits / underscores handled ✓

Note: `swift test` can't run in this sandbox (sandbox-exec blocked), so I compiled the exact logic with `swiftc` and drove it with the reported cases plus regressions — all pass. The on-disk `NoteKit.swift` matches that verified implementation.

One behavior change to be aware of: `#q#1` now yields `["q", "1"]` (a `#` inside a tag starts a new one). That's the natural reading of the two examples and preserves order; flag it if you'd rather treat a second `#` as a terminator instead.


